Fix a real upstream parallel-build-safety bug (not an AXOS feature, and
unrelated to any of the RTCONFIG_ work in earlier patches): release/src/router/Makefile's
`all: clean-build kernel_header version fsbuild $(obj-y)` lists clean-build
(which does `rm -rf $(BCM_FSBUILD_DIR)` among other staging-directory wipes)
as a co-equal prerequisite of "all" alongside fsbuild and every $(obj-y)
component target — several of which (e.g. protect_srv/lib) themselves
populate that exact same staging tree as part of their own install step.
GNU Make is free to build sibling prerequisites of one target in parallel
under -j>1, with no ordering guarantee between them unless the Makefile
says otherwise. This Makefile already works around the identical shape of
problem for the older (non-HND_ROUTER) kernel build path a bit further down
(see the LINUXDIR rules, which prepend a `.NOTPARALLEL:` line before
invoking that kernel build) — this fix applies the same, established
technique to the "all" target used for GT-AX6000 (HND_ROUTER=y).

Confirmed via two separate real -j4 build attempts, failing identically
both times: clean-build's `rm -rf` and protect_srv/lib's
`mkdir -p .../fs.build/public/...` raced, failing with "rm: cannot remove
'.../fs.build/public': Directory not empty". This did not reproduce at -j1
(no parallelism to race) — it is inherently a timing-dependent race, not
guaranteed to reproduce at every parallel -j value, though it reproduced
reliably (twice in a row) at -j4 on this host.

Fix: add a bare `.NOTPARALLEL:` directive to release/src/router/Makefile,
right before the `all:` target definitions. This is scoped to just this
Makefile's own recursive `make -C router all` invocation (GNU Make's
`.NOTPARALLEL` only affects the make process that reads the makefile
containing it) — it does not affect parallelism in the kernel build (a
separate make invocation with its own MAKEFLAGS) or within each $(obj-y)
component's own recursive sub-make (e.g. rc/Makefile compiling its many
.o files still parallelizes normally, since that is a distinct child make
process reading a different Makefile). Only the top-level sequencing of
clean-build/kernel_header/version/fsbuild/$(obj-y) against each other is
serialized — exactly the ordering this race needed.

`git apply --check`-verified against the pinned tag (3004.388.9), applied on
top of 0001-0007. Not yet verified past that point in a completed build —
see docs/build-environment.md and docs/ROADMAP.md.

diff --git a/release/src/router/Makefile b/release/src/router/Makefile
index fe6950ea62..080bf0066f 100644
--- a/release/src/router/Makefile
+++ b/release/src/router/Makefile
@@ -1850,6 +1850,23 @@ LINUX_ARCH_ASM_INCL_DIR = $(if ($KPATH_3X),$(LINUXDIR)/arch/mips/include/asm,$(L
 # Basic rules
 #
 
+# clean-build (rm -rf $(BCM_FSBUILD_DIR) among other staging dirs) is listed
+# as a co-equal prerequisite of "all" alongside fsbuild and every $(obj-y)
+# component — several of which (e.g. protect_srv/lib) themselves populate
+# that same staging tree as part of their own install step. GNU Make is free
+# to build sibling prerequisites of one target in parallel under -j>1, with
+# no ordering between them; this Makefile already uses the same .NOTPARALLEL
+# workaround for the older kernel build path below (see LINUXDIR rules) for
+# an analogous reason. Confirmed via a real -j4 build attempt: clean-build's
+# `rm -rf` and protect_srv/lib's `mkdir -p .../fs.build/public/...` raced,
+# failing with "rm: cannot remove '.../fs.build/public': Directory not
+# empty". Scoped to just this Makefile's own recursive `make -C router all`
+# invocation — it does not affect parallelism in the kernel build (a
+# separate make invocation) or within each $(obj-y) component's own
+# sub-make (e.g. rc/Makefile compiling its many .o files still parallelizes
+# normally).
+.NOTPARALLEL:
+
 ifeq ($(HND_ROUTER),y)
 all: clean-build kernel_header version fsbuild $(obj-y)
 else ifeq ($(RTCONFIG_ARM),y)
